  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nBetter coverage of showers and storms currently exists to our south \ndown across AL and GA. This activity is associated with a weak area \nof low pressure moving north toward our region. Some of this \nactivity could approach our southern most areas later this \nafternoon and evening. Elsewhere, a few isolated showers and \nstorms expected through the evening, but most areas stay dry. A \nstrong storm or two is possible for areas south of I-40 with \nstraight line damaging winds being the main threat.\n\nShortwave still set to move across the region tomorrow, providing an \nuptick in shower and storm coverage. The best coverage will be \nduring peak heating. While severe weather isn't expected, a few \nstrong storms are possible. Locally heavy rainfall remains the \nmain threat due to elevated PW values of around 2 inches. A low- \nend threat for isolated flooding exists.\n\nDrier air moves in on Sunday behind the shortwave, which means \nshowers and storms will be more isolated. We then start the new week \nwith the ridge of high pressure building back into the area. This \nwill keep us hot with above normal temperatures and very low \nchances for rain. Isolated, diurnal, showers and storms possible \nover the high terrain. Mid-to-late week, a broad upper trough and\nfrontal boundary approach from the north. This will help to nudge\nthe high southward and transition our area to more zonal flow, \nwhich will allow for rain chances to return. \n\nForecast high tomorrow for Chattanooga is 88 degrees, due to \nincreased cloud cover and showers/storms. If they do hit 90 \nthough, the 90 degree and above streak will likely continue for \nseveral to numerous more days due to low precip chances into next \nweek.",
